1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,875
Tortured by the Kempeitai, He
Went On to Build a Nation's Homes

2
00:00:04,875 --> 00:00:08,270
Sometime during the Japanese
Occupation of Singapore,

3
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:12,401
the Kempeitai picked up a young
Peranakan businessman named Lim

4
00:00:12,481 --> 00:00:16,008
Kim San — not once, but twice
— and tortured him on the

5
00:00:16,088 --> 00:00:19,614
strength of two accusations
that couldn't both be true:

6
00:00:19,694 --> 00:00:23,825
that he was a communist, and that
he was a British sympathiser.

7
00:00:23,825 --> 00:00:27,519
Two decades later, that
same man would be the one

8
00:00:27,599 --> 00:00:31,446
Singaporeans called "Mr
HDB," credited with solving

9
00:00:31,526 --> 00:00:35,300
the housing crisis of a
newly independent nation.

10
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:39,300
Almost nobody remembers
the part in between.

11
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:43,396
Lim was born in Singapore
in 1916, the eldest of six

12
00:00:43,476 --> 00:00:47,491
children in a Peranakan
Chinese family; his father,

13
00:00:47,571 --> 00:00:52,550
Lim Choon Huat, was a businessman
in shipping and commodities.

14
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:56,153
He was educated at Oldham
Hall School and the

15
00:00:56,233 --> 00:00:59,999
Anglo-Chinese School
before going on to Raffles

16
00:01:00,079 --> 00:01:03,925
College to study economics,
graduating in 1939.

17
00:01:03,925 --> 00:01:07,924
It was there that he struck up a
friendship with a fellow student

18
00:01:08,004 --> 00:01:11,940
named Goh Keng Swee — a connection
that would matter far more to

19
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:16,475
Singapore's history than either of
them could have guessed at the time.

20
00:01:16,475 --> 00:01:21,906
In February 1940 Lim married Pang Gek
Kim in a traditional Peranakan wedding,

21
00:01:21,986 --> 00:01:24,411
and afterward went to work managing

22
00:01:24,491 --> 00:01:27,775
his father-in-law's
businesses: a sago factory,

23
00:01:27,855 --> 00:01:32,650
along with interests in diamonds,
jewellery, pawnshops and banking.

24
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:34,725
Then came the war.

25
00:01:34,725 --> 00:01:39,303
When the Japanese occupied Singapore
in 1942, the secret military

26
00:01:39,383 --> 00:01:43,531
police detained and tortured
Lim on suspicion of being both

27
00:01:43,611 --> 00:01:47,616
pro-communist and pro-British
— contradictory labels that

28
00:01:47,696 --> 00:01:51,987
reflected how broadly the
Kempeitai's suspicion could fall on

29
00:01:52,067 --> 00:01:56,725
Chinese businessmen with the wrong
connections, real or imagined.

30
00:01:56,725 --> 00:02:01,010
Decades later, Lim reflected that
those who survived the "horror

31
00:02:01,090 --> 00:02:05,035
and the brutality" of the
occupation would never forget it,

32
00:02:05,115 --> 00:02:09,264
and that the experience had
politicised his entire generation,

33
00:02:09,344 --> 00:02:14,050
leaving them determined to "never
let our fate be decided by others."

34
00:02:14,050 --> 00:02:18,425
After the war, though, he didn't
go anywhere near politics.

35
00:02:18,425 --> 00:02:23,746
He went back to business, working to
make up for years lost to the occupation,

36
00:02:23,826 --> 00:02:27,417
and by 36 he'd made his
first million using a machine

37
00:02:27,497 --> 00:02:32,275
that produced sago pearls more
cheaply than anyone else in the trade.

38
00:02:32,275 --> 00:02:36,220
He stayed on the sidelines
of the People's Action Party

39
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:39,441
even as it rose —
friendly with its leaders,

40
00:02:39,521 --> 00:02:44,125
quietly supportive, but unwilling
to stand as a candidate in the

41
00:02:44,205 --> 00:02:48,450
pivotal 1959 election that
first brought the PAP to power.

42
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:52,463
What he offered instead was
administrative help, and in 1960

43
00:02:52,543 --> 00:02:56,897
the new government took him up on
it in the biggest way possible:

44
00:02:56,977 --> 00:03:00,854
chairman of the newly created
Housing & Development Board,

45
00:03:00,934 --> 00:03:05,084
tasked with clearing a backlog
that had left more than 400,000

46
00:03:05,164 --> 00:03:09,325
people crammed into slum shophouses
and squatter settlements.

47
00:03:09,325 --> 00:03:13,332
Lim took the job unpaid and
stayed unpaid for three years,

48
00:03:13,412 --> 00:03:18,546
dispensing with the Singapore Improvement
Trust's careful planning process

49
00:03:18,626 --> 00:03:22,915
in favour of rough estimates and
speed, standardising designs,

50
00:03:22,995 --> 00:03:27,575
and supervising private contractors
instead of building directly.

51
00:03:27,575 --> 00:03:31,581
When the Bukit Ho Swee fire
tore through a squatter

52
00:03:31,661 --> 00:03:37,429
settlement in May 1961 and left roughly
16,000 people homeless overnight,

53
00:03:37,509 --> 00:03:41,274
it was Lim's HDB that
rebuilt and rehoused them,

54
00:03:41,354 --> 00:03:45,600
delivering 1,200 replacement
flats within four years.

55
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:50,420
By the time his three-year tenure
was up, the Board had built around

56
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:55,897
26,000 flats — more than the SIT had
managed in its entire 32-year history —

57
00:03:55,977 --> 00:03:59,933
with the satellite town of
Queenstown, over 17,500 flats

58
00:04:00,013 --> 00:04:04,833
built as a self-contained new town
with its own shops and amenities,

59
00:04:04,913 --> 00:04:09,525
as the flagship achievement that
later HDB towns would all copy.

60
00:04:09,525 --> 00:04:13,266
Singapore awarded him
the Order of Temasek,

61
00:04:13,346 --> 00:04:16,553
its highest civilian honour, in 1962;

62
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:21,875
the Philippines gave him the
Ramon Magsaysay Award in 1965.

63
00:04:21,875 --> 00:04:26,105
By 1963, Lee Kuan Yew
had persuaded Lim to do

64
00:04:26,185 --> 00:04:30,400
what he'd refused in
1959: stand for office.

65
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:35,041
He won the Cairnhill seat and was made
Minister for National Development,

66
00:04:35,121 --> 00:04:37,111
overseeing the housing programme

67
00:04:37,191 --> 00:04:40,425
he'd just spent three years
building from scratch.

68
00:04:40,425 --> 00:04:45,690
Two years later, after Singapore's
abrupt 1965 separation from Malaysia,

69
00:04:45,770 --> 00:04:47,620
Lee moved him to Finance —

70
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:52,525
and it's here that Lim's
least-remembered achievement played out.

71
00:04:52,525 --> 00:04:56,899
Singapore had kept sharing a
common currency with Malaysia and

72
00:04:56,979 --> 00:05:02,286
Brunei after separation, but by mid-1966
the arrangement was falling apart:

73
00:05:02,366 --> 00:05:05,304
Malaysia's finance minister, Tan Siew Sin,

74
00:05:05,384 --> 00:05:10,979
wanted Bank Negara Malaysia to hold title
over the reserves backing Singapore's

75
00:05:11,059 --> 00:05:15,225
share of that currency, a
condition Lim refused to accept.

76
00:05:15,225 --> 00:05:19,400
He flew to Kuala Lumpur in August
1966 with a formal proposal —

77
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:23,655
either an independent trustee
for the reserves, or a separately

78
00:05:23,735 --> 00:05:28,180
incorporated Singapore currency
board — and when Tan rejected both,

79
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:32,233
Lim held his ground: Singapore,
he wrote back, "could not be

80
00:05:32,313 --> 00:05:36,500
placed in a position where its
reserves might be jeopardised."

81
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:40,856
Fittingly, it was his old Raffles
College friend Goh Keng Swee,

82
00:05:40,936 --> 00:05:45,725
by then Defence Minister, who worked
the negotiations alongside him.

83
00:05:45,725 --> 00:05:49,949
On the 17th of August 1966 both
governments announced they would

84
00:05:50,029 --> 00:05:53,985
issue separate currencies,
effective the 12th of June 1967 —

85
00:05:54,065 --> 00:05:57,818
Singapore's second, far quieter
separation from Malaysia,

86
00:05:57,898 --> 00:06:02,324
and one that gave the republic
direct control over its own reserves

87
00:06:02,404 --> 00:06:07,570
for the first time. (The following year,
Lim and Goh swapped portfolios again,

88
00:06:07,650 --> 00:06:10,475
trading Finance for Interior and Defence.)

89
00:06:10,475 --> 00:06:14,464
Lim went on to hold half the
seats in Cabinet over the

90
00:06:14,544 --> 00:06:17,478
following decade and a half — Education,

91
00:06:17,558 --> 00:06:23,204
Environment twice over, Communications,
National Development a second time —

92
00:06:23,284 --> 00:06:26,750
before retiring from
elected politics in 1980.

93
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:31,275
He wasn't done, though: he chaired
the Port of Singapore Authority

94
00:06:31,355 --> 00:06:35,391
through the 1980s, ran the
Monetary Authority of Singapore,

95
00:06:35,471 --> 00:06:39,786
later took over Singapore Press
Holdings as executive chairman,

96
00:06:39,866 --> 00:06:44,750
and spent over a decade chairing the
Council of Presidential Advisers.

97
00:06:44,750 --> 00:06:48,056
He died in Singapore in July 2006, at 89,

98
00:06:48,136 --> 00:06:53,588
having spent six decades in public
service, from the HDB's earliest

99
00:06:53,668 --> 00:06:58,375
years to some of the government's
highest advisory posts.

100
00:06:58,375 --> 00:07:03,377
Why it matters today: Every account
of Lim Kim San leads with the HDB,

101
00:07:03,457 --> 00:07:06,716
and it's not wrong to —
a fifth of Singapore's

102
00:07:06,796 --> 00:07:11,225
population now lives in the
system he built the template for.

103
00:07:11,225 --> 00:07:15,630
But the housing crisis wasn't the
first emergency he'd lived through,

104
00:07:15,710 --> 00:07:18,360
and the currency board wasn't the last one

105
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:22,195
he'd help build from nothing;
both were downstream of a war

106
00:07:22,275 --> 00:07:26,615
that convinced an entire generation
of Singaporeans that competence,

107
00:07:26,695 --> 00:07:29,800
not just survival, was
the only insurance against

108
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:33,325
ever again having their fate
decided by someone else.
